window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 — Health, Environment, and Society","h":"","i":"ccs-105-health-environment-and-society","x":"CCS 105 — Health, Environment, and Society University of Embu · Biological Sciences This pack organizes the available course outline and lecture materials into a topic-by-topic study path. Search covers the text inside each note. Use the progress tracker to mark a topic complete only after you can explain it without looking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 — Health, Environment, and Society","h":"Learning goals","i":"learning-goals","x":"Learning goals Work through each topic in order, or search for a specific term or example. Use the interactive recall checklist at the end of each note. Record confidence and revision dates in the progress tracker. Consult the source material when a note marks a gap or an equation needs checking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 — Health, Environment, and Society","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes 01 · Holistic Wellness 02 · Communicable Diseases 03 · Noncommunicable Diseases 04 · Mental Health 05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya 06 · Hygiene and Sanitation 07 · Climate Change 08 · Digital Environment 09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment 10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya"},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 — Health, Environment, and Society","h":"How to study","i":"how-to-study","x":"How to study Read one topic and mark its checklist as you complete each activity. Close the notes and write the key ideas from memory. Use search or “Find in note” to resolve specific gaps. Update your confidence and next review date in the progress tracker."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"","i":"your-study-plan","x":"Your study plan"},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Weekly study cycle","i":"weekly-study-cycle","x":"Weekly study cycle Preview the course outline and choose this week's topics. Study one or two notes per session. Do a closed-book recall after each note. Revisit weak topics within 48 hours. Complete a mixed-topic review at the end of the week."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Exam-week cycle","i":"exam-week-cycle","x":"Exam-week cycle Review the progress tracker and prioritize low-confidence topics. Rework examples without looking at their solutions. Search the notes for definitions and distinctions you cannot recall. Complete the question bank and record errors in the tracker. Protect time for sleep and a final light review."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 · Exam-day review","h":"","i":"ccs-105-exam-day-review","x":"CCS 105 · Exam-day review Use this as a rapid recall map. For a full-mark response: define → explain → apply an example → note a limitation or context ."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 · Exam-day review","h":"Health and wellbeing","i":"health-and-wellbeing","x":"Health and wellbeing Holistic wellness: connected physical, emotional, social, intellectual, spiritual, occupational, environmental, cultural, economic, and climate-related dimensions. Communicable disease: infectious agent can spread; break the chain at source, route, or susceptible host. NCDs: generally not spread person to person; risks interact with biology, behaviour, society, and environment. Mental health: continuum shaped by individual, relationship, community, and structural factors; respond supportively and seek qualified help when needed. Substance-related harm: use non-stigmatizing language; prevention and recovery combine personal, community, and clinical support. Hygiene and sanitation: safe water + hand/food hygiene + toilets + waste systems; infrastructure matters."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 · Exam-day review","h":"Environment and civic life","i":"environment-and-civic-life","x":"Environment and civic life Mitigation reduces greenhouse-gas emissions / increases carbon storage; adaptation reduces harm from climate impacts. Digital safety: verify requests, protect credentials, manage privacy, check information and AI outputs, consider e-waste. Kenya Constitution Article 10: know examples of rule of law, participation, accountability, inclusion, integrity, and sustainable development."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 · Exam-day review","h":"Society and livelihoods","i":"society-and-livelihoods","x":"Society and livelihoods Unemployment types: frictional (between jobs), structural (skills/location mismatch), cyclical (economic downturn), seasonal (time-of-year work). Poverty is multidimensional; discuss interacting causes and evaluate responses by fair access, decent work, and sustainability."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 · Exam-day review","h":"Five high-value answer habits","i":"five-high-value-answer-habits","x":"Five high-value answer habits Define terms before giving examples. Compare concepts explicitly (for example, adaptation versus mitigation). Explain mechanisms, not just lists. Avoid absolute claims and blame; include social determinants and context. Use current official sources for changing law, statistics, or health guidance. Practise the topic-linked question bank , compare with the answer key , and log weak areas in the progress tracker ."},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 · Topic progress and weak-point log","h":"","i":"ccs-105-topic-progress-and-weak-point-log","x":"CCS 105 · Topic progress and weak-point log After attempting the linked questions closed-book, score 0–3 for each topic and rate confidence 1–5 . Set a specific review action and date; focus first on low scores or low confidence. Topic Note Questions Score /3 Confidence /5 Weak point / next action / review date 01 · Holistic wellness Note H01-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 02 · Communicable diseases Note H02-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 03 · NCDs Note H03-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 04 · Mental health Note H04-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 05 · Substance use Note H05-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 06 · Hygiene and sanitation Note H06-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 07 · Climate change Note H07-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 08 · Digital environment Note H08-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 09 · National values Note H09-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 10 · Unemployment and poverty Note H10-Q1 , Q2 , Q3 Act on the result: 3/3 with confidence 4–5 → delayed mixed recall; 2/3 or confidence 2–3 → revisit the named gap and retry missed questions; 0–1/3 or confidence 1 → study the note, explain it aloud, then retry all three."},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 · Topic progress and weak-point log","h":"Weak-point action log","i":"weak-point-action-log","x":"Weak-point action log Date found Topic / question What I could not explain Next action Retry date / result"},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"","i":"01-holistic-wellness","x":"01 · Holistic Wellness"},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Core idea","i":"core-idea","x":"Core idea Holistic wellness views health as the interaction of a whole person with their relationships, community, work, culture, and environment. It is broader than simply having no illness. The dimensions overlap: poor sleep, for example, can affect physical health, mood, concentration, and relationships."},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Dimensions to know","i":"dimensions-to-know","x":"Dimensions to know Dimension What it concerns A practical example Physical Movement, nutrition, rest, prevention, and appropriate care Regular activity, balanced meals, and seeking care when needed Emotional / psychological Recognizing feelings, coping, self-esteem, and purpose Naming stress and choosing a safe coping strategy Social Supportive relationships and participation in community Maintaining friendships and asking for support Intellectual Curiosity, learning, critical thinking, and applying knowledge Learning a skill and evaluating evidence Spiritual Meaning, values, purpose, and connection Reflecting on values; spirituality need not be religious Occupational Meaning, satisfaction, skills, and balance in work or study Setting boundaries between study and rest Environmental Safe, supportive surroundings and care for shared resources Clean water, safe housing, and community clean-up Cultural Connection to identity, traditions, and respectful belonging Maintaining meaningful cultural ties without excluding others Economic Financial security, resources, and economic conditions Budgeting while recognizing structural barriers to income Climate-related Effects of climate and"},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Dimensions to know","i":"dimensions-to-know","x":"Economic Financial security, resources, and economic conditions Budgeting while recognizing structural barriers to income Climate-related Effects of climate and extreme weather on wellbeing Heat, floods, or drought affecting health and livelihoods These are useful lenses, not isolated boxes. A person’s wellbeing is also shaped by social determinants such as education, housing, income, access to health services, and safety."},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Apply the idea","i":"apply-the-idea","x":"Apply the idea If a student is exhausted and falling behind, a holistic response asks about more than motivation: sleep, workload, health, finances, relationships, the learning environment, and available support may all matter. Choose a response that addresses the relevant causes without blaming the person."},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Explain why wellness is broader than absence of disease. Name four dimensions and give a distinct example for each. Give one example of how two dimensions can influence each other. Self-check: A strong answer defines the dimension and explains the example. Avoid treating the dimensions as independent or suggesting that every person has identical needs. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"","i":"02-communicable-diseases","x":"02 · Communicable Diseases"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Meaning and transmission","i":"meaning-and-transmission","x":"Meaning and transmission A communicable disease is an illness caused by an infectious agent that can be transmitted, directly or indirectly, from a source to a susceptible person. Agents include viruses, bacteria, fungi, and parasites. Infection does not always produce symptoms, and symptoms alone do not identify the cause."},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"The chain of infection","i":"the-chain-of-infection","x":"The chain of infection Think of spread as a chain: infectious agent → reservoir/source → exit route → transmission route → entry route → susceptible host . Prevention interrupts one or more links. Route Examples of interruption Respiratory droplets or aerosols Ventilation, staying home when ill, appropriate masks, cough etiquette Contaminated food or water (faecal–oral) Safe water, handwashing, sanitation, food hygiene Blood or sexual contact Testing, sterile equipment, safer sex, and appropriate protective measures Vectors such as mosquitoes Removing standing water, nets, repellents, and local vector control Direct contact or contaminated surfaces Hand hygiene, cleaning, and avoiding shared contaminated items"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Prevention and response","i":"prevention-and-response","x":"Prevention and response Primary prevention: reduce the chance of infection (for example vaccination, safe water, or vector control). Early detection and care: seek qualified assessment, testing, and treatment; follow public-health guidance. Limit further spread: use appropriate isolation or precautions, inform relevant contacts when advised, and complete prescribed treatment. Community measures: surveillance, health education, sanitation, access to care, and timely risk communication."},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Exam distinctions","i":"exam-distinctions","x":"Exam distinctions Infection means an infectious agent is present; disease refers to illness or harmful effects, though infection may be asymptomatic. A vector carries an agent between hosts; a reservoir is where an agent normally lives or multiplies. Antibiotics treat certain bacterial infections, not viral infections; only a qualified clinician should advise treatment."},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Reproduce the chain of infection and explain two points where spread can be interrupted. Classify handwashing, vaccination, and mosquito-net use as prevention measures and explain what each targets. Why is it unsafe to infer that a disease is bacterial from symptoms alone? Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"","i":"03-noncommunicable-diseases","x":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"What makes a disease noncommunicable?","i":"what-makes-a-disease-noncommunicable","x":"What makes a disease noncommunicable? Noncommunicable diseases (NCDs) are generally long-duration conditions that are not passed from person to person through ordinary infectious transmission. Major groups include cardiovascular diseases, cancers, chronic respiratory diseases, and diabetes. They often result from interacting biological, behavioural, social, commercial, and environmental factors."},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Risk and protection","i":"risk-and-protection","x":"Risk and protection Risk factors can be modifiable (such as tobacco use, harmful alcohol use, inactivity, unhealthy diets, and some environmental exposures) or non-modifiable (such as age, inherited susceptibility, or family history). Risk is not blame: income, food access, safe places to exercise, education, and health services shape what choices are available."},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Prevention levels","i":"prevention-levels","x":"Prevention levels Population prevention: make healthier environments easier (clean air, safe activity spaces, health education, and accessible services). Individual risk reduction: avoid tobacco, choose feasible nutritious foods and activity, and seek preventive care. Screening and early diagnosis: use recommended services; screening is not a diagnosis and should be interpreted by health professionals. Treatment and long-term support: follow an agreed care plan and address physical, mental, and social needs."},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"A useful exam structure","i":"a-useful-exam-structure","x":"A useful exam structure For an NCD question, define the condition/group, name risk factors and protective actions, explain prevention or management, and acknowledge social determinants. Avoid claiming that one factor inevitably causes disease or that lifestyle alone explains every case."},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Distinguish an NCD from an infectious disease. Give two modifiable and two non-modifiable risk factors. Explain why screening and diagnosis are not the same thing. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"","i":"04-mental-health","x":"04 · Mental Health"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Understand the concept","i":"understand-the-concept","x":"Understand the concept Mental health is part of health and wellbeing: it affects how people think, feel, relate to others, cope with stress, learn, and make decisions. It exists on a continuum and can change over time. A mental health condition is not a character flaw, and distress should not be diagnosed from a brief description or stereotype."},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Influences and support","i":"influences-and-support","x":"Influences and support Mental health is shaped by interacting factors: biology and physical health; life events and coping resources; relationships and community; safety, discrimination, housing, education, and income; and access to appropriate services. Protective supports can include trusted relationships, sleep and activity routines, coping skills, belonging, and timely professional care."},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Helpful response","i":"helpful-response","x":"Helpful response Listen respectfully, ask what support would help, and avoid judgment or pressure. Encourage contact with a qualified health professional or locally available support service when distress persists or disrupts daily life. In an immediate safety crisis, seek urgent local emergency or clinical help and involve a trusted person if safe to do so. Protect privacy, except where disclosure is necessary to address an immediate risk under applicable rules."},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Common misconceptions","i":"common-misconceptions","x":"Common misconceptions Mental health is not only the absence of mental illness. People can experience mental illness and still study, work, and contribute. “Just be positive” is not a treatment plan. Support should be respectful, evidence-informed, and suited to the person."},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Explain the mental-health continuum in your own words. Name three different levels of influence on mental wellbeing. How could a student respond supportively to a friend who says they are struggling? Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Use in Kenya","h":"","i":"05-drugs-and-substance-use-in-kenya","x":"05 · Drugs and Substance Use in Kenya"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Use in Kenya","h":"Key terms","i":"key-terms","x":"Key terms Psychoactive substances affect mood, perception, behaviour, or cognition. Substance use ranges from use without a diagnosed disorder to harmful use and dependence; terms should be used carefully and without stigma. Substance-use disorder is a health condition assessed by qualified professionals, not a label to apply to someone from a single behaviour."},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Use in Kenya","h":"Why use and harm occur","i":"why-use-and-harm-occur","x":"Why use and harm occur Possible influences include availability, peer and family environments, stress or trauma, mental and physical health, economic conditions, social norms, and individual factors. No single cause explains every person’s experience. Alcohol, tobacco/nicotine, and other drugs have different effects; risks depend on substance, amount, frequency, route, and the person’s health."},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Use in Kenya","h":"Consequences and support","i":"consequences-and-support","x":"Consequences and support Potential harms include injury, dependence, overdose, effects on physical or mental health, disrupted study or work, family stress, financial hardship, and legal consequences. Do not assume every person experiences all harms. A compassionate, practical response prioritizes safety and access to care."},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Use in Kenya","h":"Prevention and recovery","i":"prevention-and-recovery","x":"Prevention and recovery Prevention combines accurate education, supportive families and communities, life skills, reduced harmful availability, and accessible services. Treatment may involve clinical assessment, counselling, rehabilitation, medication where appropriate, and peer or family support. Recovery is individual and may include setbacks; avoid shame and encourage qualified care. Suspected overdose or immediate danger requires urgent local emergency medical assistance."},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Use in Kenya","h":"Kenya: law and policy","i":"kenya-law-and-policy","x":"Kenya: law and policy The course source discusses Kenyan laws and policies that regulate alcohol and control narcotic drugs and psychotropic substances, including the Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances (Control) Act. Laws can change and details depend on the conduct and current legislation; consult current official Kenyan sources rather than memorizing an outdated penalty or treating substance use as synonymous with crime."},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Use in Kenya","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Distinguish substance use, harmful use, and a clinically assessed disorder. Explain three interacting factors that may contribute to substance-related harm. Outline a non-stigmatizing prevention and support response. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"","i":"06-hygiene-and-sanitation","x":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"The public-health purpose","i":"the-public-health-purpose","x":"The public-health purpose Hygiene means practices that help maintain health and reduce exposure to disease. Sanitation concerns safe systems and conditions for managing human waste and maintaining a clean environment. Safe water, hand hygiene, food safety, toilets, drainage, and waste management work together."},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Practical prevention chain","i":"practical-prevention-chain","x":"Practical prevention chain Water: protect sources, treat or boil water when advised, and store it in clean covered containers. Hands: wash with soap and safe water at key times, including after toilet use and before preparing or eating food. Food: keep preparation areas clean, separate raw and cooked foods, cook thoroughly, and store safely. Waste and toilets: use and maintain safe sanitation; dispose of waste appropriately and prevent contamination of water sources. Community environment: control stagnant water and pests, maintain drainage, and use safe cleaning methods."},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Why infrastructure matters","i":"why-infrastructure-matters","x":"Why infrastructure matters Hygiene is not only an individual responsibility. Reliable water, accessible toilets, waste collection, safe housing, and public education shape whether healthy practices are possible. Effective sanitation protects households and the wider community."},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Distinguish hygiene from sanitation. Describe a sequence of actions that reduces food- or water-borne illness. Explain why health education alone cannot replace safe water and sanitation infrastructure. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change: Adaptation and Mitigation","h":"","i":"07-climate-change-adaptation-and-mitigation","x":"07 · Climate Change: Adaptation and Mitigation"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change: Adaptation and Mitigation","h":"Two essential responses","i":"two-essential-responses","x":"Two essential responses Climate change is a long-term change in climate patterns, including changes driven by human greenhouse-gas emissions. Mitigation reduces emissions or increases carbon storage to limit future warming. Adaptation reduces vulnerability and prepares people and systems for actual or expected climate effects. The two approaches complement one another. Challenge Adaptation example Mitigation example Drought and unreliable rainfall Drought planning, water conservation, resilient crops Efficient energy and lower-emission land use Flooding Early warning, drainage, safer land-use planning Reduce greenhouse-gas emissions Heat Shade, cooling plans, occupational precautions Energy efficiency and cleaner power"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change: Adaptation and Mitigation","h":"Equity and resilience","i":"equity-and-resilience","x":"Equity and resilience Climate impacts are not shared equally. Exposure and ability to recover depend on income, housing, health, location, livelihoods, infrastructure, and public services. Effective plans use local knowledge, reliable information, inclusive decision-making, and attention to people most at risk."},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change: Adaptation and Mitigation","h":"Exam-ready comparison","i":"exam-ready-comparison","x":"Exam-ready comparison Ask: Does this action address the cause of climate change (mitigation), or reduce harm from its effects (adaptation)? Some actions can do both, but explain the pathway rather than simply naming the activity."},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change: Adaptation and Mitigation","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Define mitigation and adaptation and give one example of each. Explain how climate hazards can affect health and livelihoods. Why must climate planning consider vulnerability and equity? Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"","i":"08-digital-environment","x":"08 · Digital Environment"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"What it includes","i":"what-it-includes","x":"What it includes The digital environment is the connected space of devices, networks, platforms, applications, content, and users through which people communicate, learn, work, and access services. A digital footprint is the information created or shared through online activity; some traces are visible and others are collected in the background."},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Participate safely and thoughtfully","i":"participate-safely-and-thoughtfully","x":"Participate safely and thoughtfully Privacy: share only what is needed; review permissions and audience settings. Security: use unique passwords, multi-factor authentication when available, updates, and caution with links or attachments. Scams and fraud: check sender identity and requests through a separate trusted channel; do not disclose credentials or one-time codes. Digital wellbeing: set boundaries, check information before sharing, and seek help for harmful online conduct. AI: verify important outputs, protect personal information, check sources and bias, and disclose or cite use when course rules require it. Environment: devices and data services consume materials and energy; extend device life, repair where practical, and use responsible e-waste channels."},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Evaluate a digital claim","i":"evaluate-a-digital-claim","x":"Evaluate a digital claim Check who published it, what evidence supports it, when it was updated, what purpose it serves, and whether independent reliable sources agree. Popularity or polished presentation is not proof."},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Define a digital footprint and give one way to manage it. List three checks before responding to an unexpected online payment or password request. Explain why AI-generated information should be verified before use. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"","i":"09-national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment","x":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Civic foundations","i":"civic-foundations","x":"Civic foundations National values are shared principles intended to guide public life and institutions. Kenya’s Constitution, Article 10, identifies national values and principles of governance including patriotism, national unity, sharing and devolution of power, the rule of law, democracy and participation of the people, human dignity, equity, social justice, inclusiveness, equality, human rights, non-discrimination, protection of marginalized groups, good governance, integrity, transparency, accountability, and sustainable development ."},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Apply, do not merely list","i":"apply-do-not-merely-list","x":"Apply, do not merely list Value Meaning in practice Rule of law Public power and citizens’ conduct are governed by law, applied fairly Participation People can take part in public decisions through lawful processes Accountability and transparency Decisions and use of public resources can be explained and scrutinized Equity and inclusion Fair access and attention to different needs and historical barriers National unity Shared civic belonging while respecting diversity and devolution Integrity Honest conduct and resistance to abuse of entrusted power"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Political environment","i":"political-environment","x":"Political environment The political environment includes institutions, laws, public participation, leadership, policy choices, and relationships among national and county levels of government. Devolution brings some decision-making and services closer to communities; citizens can engage through elections, public participation, lawful advocacy, and oversight."},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Name four Article 10 values and explain each with an example. How can public participation support accountable governance? Distinguish national unity from uniformity. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"","i":"10-unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya","x":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Definitions and types","i":"definitions-and-types","x":"Definitions and types Unemployment generally refers to people who are available for and seeking work but do not have employment; statistical definitions specify how availability and job search are measured. It differs from underemployment , where people’s work or hours do not fully use their capacity or needs. Type Main mechanism Example Frictional Time spent moving between jobs or entering the labour market A graduate searching for a first suitable role Structural Skills or location mismatch as the economy changes Workers needing retraining after a sector changes Cyclical Lower demand during an economic downturn Firms reduce staffing when sales fall Seasonal Work is available only in particular seasons Some agricultural or tourism jobs"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Poverty and its drivers","i":"poverty-and-its-drivers","x":"Poverty and its drivers Poverty involves deprivation of resources and opportunities, not only low income. Its causes can include limited decent work, unequal access to education and services, health shocks, discrimination, insecurity, economic downturns, and environmental shocks. These causes can reinforce one another."},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Responses","i":"responses","x":"Responses Strategies discussed in the course include quality education and skills development, career guidance, entrepreneurship support, access to finance and markets, infrastructure, fair labour conditions, social protection, and policies that support job creation. Evaluate a strategy by asking who benefits, whether decent work is created, whether access is fair, and whether outcomes can be sustained."},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Distinguish frictional, structural, cyclical, and seasonal unemployment. Explain two ways unemployment and poverty can reinforce one another. Evaluate one job-creation strategy using access, job quality, and sustainability. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"","i":"ccs-105-topic-linked-question-bank","x":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank Attempt closed-book. Include definitions, reasoning, and examples; use the answer key to check, then record results in the progress tracker ."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"01 · Holistic wellness","i":"01-holistic-wellness","x":"01 · Holistic wellness"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H01-Q1","i":"h01-q1","x":"H01-Q1 Define holistic wellness and explain why it is broader than absence of disease."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H01-Q2","i":"h01-q2","x":"H01-Q2 Name four dimensions of wellness and give one example of each."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H01-Q3","i":"h01-q3","x":"H01-Q3 Explain one way social or environmental conditions can affect individual wellbeing."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"02 · Communicable diseases","i":"02-communicable-diseases","x":"02 · Communicable diseases"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H02-Q1","i":"h02-q1","x":"H02-Q1 Outline the chain of infection and identify two links that prevention can interrupt."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H02-Q2","i":"h02-q2","x":"H02-Q2 Compare transmission through contaminated water with vector-borne transmission; give one prevention measure for each."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H02-Q3","i":"h02-q3","x":"H02-Q3 Explain why symptoms alone cannot reliably establish whether an infection is bacterial or viral."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"03 · Noncommunicable diseases","i":"03-noncommunicable-diseases","x":"03 · Noncommunicable diseases"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H03-Q1","i":"h03-q1","x":"H03-Q1 Define an NCD and name three major groups."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H03-Q2","i":"h03-q2","x":"H03-Q2 Distinguish modifiable from non-modifiable risk factors, with two examples of each."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H03-Q3","i":"h03-q3","x":"H03-Q3 Explain why screening is not the same as diagnosis and why social conditions matter in prevention."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"04 · Mental health","i":"04-mental-health","x":"04 · Mental health"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H04-Q1","i":"h04-q1","x":"H04-Q1 Explain the idea of mental health as a continuum."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H04-Q2","i":"h04-q2","x":"H04-Q2 Describe three interacting influences on mental wellbeing."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H04-Q3","i":"h04-q3","x":"H04-Q3 A friend says they are struggling. Give a respectful response and explain when professional support may be appropriate."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"05 · Substance use","i":"05-substance-use","x":"05 · Substance use"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H05-Q1","i":"h05-q1","x":"H05-Q1 Explain why substance use, harmful use, and a clinically assessed substance-use disorder should not be used interchangeably."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H05-Q2","i":"h05-q2","x":"H05-Q2 Discuss three factors that can contribute to substance-related harm."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H05-Q3","i":"h05-q3","x":"H05-Q3 Outline a non-stigmatizing prevention and support approach, including what to do in an immediate emergency."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"06 · Hygiene and sanitation","i":"06-hygiene-and-sanitation","x":"06 · Hygiene and sanitation"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H06-Q1","i":"h06-q1","x":"H06-Q1 Distinguish hygiene from sanitation."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H06-Q2","i":"h06-q2","x":"H06-Q2 Describe four linked practices or systems that reduce food- and water-related illness."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H06-Q3","i":"h06-q3","x":"H06-Q3 Explain why individual hygiene messages cannot replace safe water, toilets, and waste systems."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"07 · Climate change","i":"07-climate-change","x":"07 · Climate change"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H07-Q1","i":"h07-q1","x":"H07-Q1 Define mitigation and adaptation and give a distinct example of each."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H07-Q2","i":"h07-q2","x":"H07-Q2 Explain two ways climate hazards can affect health or livelihoods."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H07-Q3","i":"h07-q3","x":"H07-Q3 Why should climate adaptation planning consider vulnerability and equity?"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"08 · Digital environment","i":"08-digital-environment","x":"08 · Digital environment"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H08-Q1","i":"h08-q1","x":"H08-Q1 Define the digital environment and a digital footprint."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H08-Q2","i":"h08-q2","x":"H08-Q2 Give three checks to make before responding to an unexpected online request for money or credentials."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H08-Q3","i":"h08-q3","x":"H08-Q3 Explain two limitations or risks of relying on an AI-generated answer for coursework."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"09 · National values and civic life","i":"09-national-values-and-civic-life","x":"09 · National values and civic life"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H09-Q1","i":"h09-q1","x":"H09-Q1 Name four values in Article 10 of Kenya’s Constitution and explain each briefly."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H09-Q2","i":"h09-q2","x":"H09-Q2 Explain how public participation and accountability can reinforce one another."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H09-Q3","i":"h09-q3","x":"H09-Q3 Distinguish national unity from uniformity in a diverse, devolved country."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"10 · Unemployment and poverty","i":"10-unemployment-and-poverty","x":"10 · Unemployment and poverty"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H10-Q1","i":"h10-q1","x":"H10-Q1 Compare frictional, structural, cyclical, and seasonal unemployment."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H10-Q2","i":"h10-q2","x":"H10-Q2 Explain two ways unemployment and poverty may reinforce one another."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Topic-linked question bank","h":"H10-Q3","i":"h10-q3","x":"H10-Q3 Evaluate one job-creation strategy using access, job quality, and sustainability."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Answer guide","h":"","i":"ccs-105-answer-guide","x":"CCS 105 · Answer guide Use these as marking points, not scripts to memorize. For each answer, check accuracy, explanation, relevant example, and appropriate qualification."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Answer guide","h":"01 · Holistic wellness","i":"01-holistic-wellness","x":"01 · Holistic wellness H01-Q1: Wellness considers interconnected physical, emotional, social, intellectual, spiritual, occupational, environmental, cultural, economic, and climate-related factors, not only whether illness is present. H01-Q2: Any four dimensions with distinct, accurate examples; e.g., physical—activity/rest; social—supportive relationships; intellectual—learning; environmental—safe water. H01-Q3: Explain a pathway: unsafe housing can increase exposure to hazards; low income can limit access to nutritious food or care. Avoid implying individual choice alone explains outcomes."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Answer guide","h":"02 · Communicable diseases","i":"02-communicable-diseases","x":"02 · Communicable diseases H02-Q1: Agent, reservoir/source, exit, transmission, entry, susceptible host. Interrupt with vaccination, sanitation, ventilation, hand hygiene, vector control, or other relevant measure. H02-Q2: Water-borne spread can be reduced with safe water, sanitation, and food hygiene; vector-borne spread with nets, repellents, and vector control. H02-Q3: Many illnesses share symptoms; diagnosis may require history, examination, or tests. Antibiotics do not treat viruses; qualified clinicians guide care."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Answer guide","h":"03 · Noncommunicable diseases","i":"03-noncommunicable-diseases","x":"03 · Noncommunicable diseases H03-Q1: Conditions generally not transmitted person to person; examples include cardiovascular disease, cancer, chronic respiratory disease, and diabetes. H03-Q2: Modifiable examples: tobacco exposure, inactivity; non-modifiable: age, inherited susceptibility. These alter risk; they do not guarantee an outcome. H03-Q3: Screening identifies possible risk/early signs and may require follow-up; diagnosis is a clinical conclusion. Access, income, environment, and services shape prevention."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Answer guide","h":"04 · Mental health","i":"04-mental-health","x":"04 · Mental health H04-Q1: Wellbeing varies over time and circumstance; a person can experience distress without a diagnosed disorder, and support needs vary. H04-Q2: Any three levels, such as physical health, coping and life events, relationships, community belonging, discrimination, income, or service access. H04-Q3: Listen without judgment, ask what help is wanted, encourage qualified support if distress persists or impairs life; urgent safety risk calls for immediate local emergency/clinical help."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Answer guide","h":"05 · Substance use","i":"05-substance-use","x":"05 · Substance use H05-Q1: These terms describe different patterns and levels of harm; disorder requires qualified assessment, and stigmatizing labels are not diagnoses. H05-Q2: Interacting influences can include availability, stress/trauma, peer setting, health, economic conditions, and service access. H05-Q3: Combine accurate education, supportive community/family, harm prevention, and accessible clinical care. Suspected overdose or immediate danger requires urgent medical help."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Answer guide","h":"06 · Hygiene and sanitation","i":"06-hygiene-and-sanitation","x":"06 · Hygiene and sanitation H06-Q1: Hygiene is health-protective practice; sanitation is safe management of waste and environmental conditions. H06-Q2: Examples: safe water/storage, handwashing, safe food handling, toilets, drainage, and waste disposal. H06-Q3: People cannot reliably practise safe routines without enabling infrastructure; community systems prevent contamination beyond one household."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Answer guide","h":"07 · Climate change","i":"07-climate-change","x":"07 · Climate change H07-Q1: Mitigation addresses emissions/carbon storage; adaptation prepares for or reduces harm from climate effects. Examples must show the relevant mechanism. H07-Q2: Heat can raise health risks and reduce outdoor work; drought/flood can disrupt water, food, housing, or income. H07-Q3: Exposure and recovery capacity differ by location, income, health, housing, and services; plans should include affected communities and distribute protection fairly."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Answer guide","h":"08 · Digital environment","i":"08-digital-environment","x":"08 · Digital environment H08-Q1: Connected devices, networks, applications, content, and users; footprint is information generated or shared through online activity. H08-Q2: Verify identity via another channel; inspect the request and destination; never share passwords or one-time codes; use official contact details. H08-Q3: AI may fabricate facts or citations, reflect bias, or mishandle private data. Verify against credible sources and follow course disclosure rules."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Answer guide","h":"09 · National values and civic life","i":"09-national-values-and-civic-life","x":"09 · National values and civic life H09-Q1: Any four Article 10 values with explanation (e.g., rule of law means public authority acts under law; participation gives people a role in decisions). H09-Q2: Participation surfaces public needs and scrutiny; transparency/accountability allow officials to explain decisions and respond to concerns. H09-Q3: Unity is shared civic belonging and cooperation; uniformity would erase differences. Unity can respect cultural diversity and devolution."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CCS 105 · Answer guide","h":"10 · Unemployment and poverty","i":"10-unemployment-and-poverty","x":"10 · Unemployment and poverty H10-Q1: Frictional—transition/search; structural—skills/location mismatch; cyclical—economic downturn; seasonal—work varies by season. H10-Q2: Job loss lowers income and access to essentials; poverty can restrict education, transport, or tools needed to find work. These are possible pathways, not inevitabilities. H10-Q3: Name a strategy, explain how it can create or enable decent work, consider who can access it, identify risks/limits, and assess long-term viability."}];